\paragraph{Context and investigated scope.} The complete supplied definition, including source qualifications and the dated literature assessment, was reviewed. The calculation below is an elementary restricted attempt motivated by [S2]; it does not claim novelty or a fresh literature-status determination.

\paragraph{A finite-dimensional gauge slice.} Let $V^0\xrightarrow{d_0}V^1\xrightarrow{d_1}V^2$ be a finite-dimensional Hilbert complex, so $d_1d_0=0$. Infinitesimal deformations obey $d_1a=0$, with gauge changes $a\mapsto a+d_0u$. Orthogonal projection onto $\operatorname{im}d_0$ decomposes $a=d_0u+h$, where $d_0^*h=0$. Since $d_1d_0=0$, also $d_1h=0$. Thus every infinitesimal gauge class has a unique representative in
\[
 \mathcal H^1=\ker d_1\cap\ker d_0^*.
\]
Uniqueness follows because an element simultaneously in $\operatorname{im}d_0$ and its orthogonal complement is zero.

\paragraph{Second-order obstruction survives gauge fixing.} Suppose an antisymmetric graded bracket defines a Maurer--Cartan equation $da+\tfrac12[a,a]=0$. For $a(t)=th+t^2b+O(t^3)$ with $dh=0$, the next equation is $db=-\tfrac12[h,h]$. Therefore the degree-two cohomology class of $[h,h]$ must vanish. This requirement is not removed by imposing a linear slice. For an explicit failure take $V^1=\mathbb C h$, $V^2=\mathbb C z$, zero differential and $[h,h]=z$, with all other brackets zero. The graded Jacobi identity holds because $z$ is central, but no nonzero first-order direction extends to second order.

\paragraph{Original-target gap.} This toy differential graded Lie algebra is not identified with the source's coupled quantum-vertex-algebra deformation complex. Establishing an analytic adjoint, a useful propagator and compatible nonlinear gauge in that complex is the first gap. Interpreting its surviving fields as physical degrees of freedom is an additional question; the report's proposed fivebrane interpretation is not assumed.

\paragraph{Verification and adversarial audit.}
Checked unique harmonic representative, degree signs and the central-bracket Jacobi identity; distinguished linear gauge fixing from unobstructed nonlinear deformation. Model self-audit only; no independent human proof checking. Prompts A and B were applied to this canonical record and its qualified source scope. Checked unique harmonic representative, degree signs and the central-bracket Jacobi identity; distinguished linear gauge fixing from unobstructed nonlinear deformation. Retained unresolved status and zero original-target progress; The actual quantum-vertex-algebra complex lacks a constructed compatible analytic gauge/propagator and physical state identification.

Coordinator checked the orthogonal gauge slice and central degree-two obstruction example; odd degree permits the nonzero bracket [h,h]. This additional check was model-assisted, not independent human verification.

\paragraph{Tools, process and contributions.}
Python 3 was used for file preparation and structural checks; no numerical experiment or theorem prover was used. Contribution prompt A was followed by prompt B. GPT-6 Astra with ultra reasoning drafted the research and performed its stated self-audit; any additional coordinator contribution is identified in the verification and audit. A GPT-6-based Codex coordinator assembled the manuscript and checked repository integration; its exact variant and reasoning configuration were not exposed. No human mathematical contribution, independent human proof check or formal proof certification is supplied. Complete derivations and the consulted source references are included here; private reasoning transcripts are not published.

\paragraph{Final status and first remaining gap.}
\textbf{UNRESOLVED}. The actual quantum-vertex-algebra complex lacks a constructed compatible analytic gauge/propagator and physical state identification.

